      <h1 class="page-header">Entangled Ecologies</h1>

      <figure class="align-right">
        <img src="{{ '/assets/images/regrowth-i-davidberg-0.jpg' | url }}" alt="A young Black woman looks to the camera among tree debris in this black-and-white photo" width="476" height="720">
        <figcaption>David Berg, <em>Regrowth II, </em>2017. Photograph, St Croix.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="curator-msg float-left">
        <p>The St. Lucian poet and Nobel Prize winner Derek Walcott writes that Caribbean history &ldquo;is there in Antillean geography, in the vegetation itself.&rdquo;<sup>1</sup></p>

        <p>In this pathway, artists, musicians, and poets tell the history of Hurricane Mar&iacute;a through the transformation of the island&rsquo;s trees.</p>

        <p>These trees bear witness to and share in the story of its people: both are a mix of indigenous and transplanted varieties, many felled by the storm, some uprooted, still more learning to flourish in the spaces where others have fallen.</p>

        <p>Part elegy, part celebration of regrowth, these works reveal to us that the lives and landscapes of Puerto Rico are deeply and beautifully entangled.</p>

        <p><sup>1</sup>Derek Walcott, &ldquo;The Antilles, Fragments of Epic Memory: The 1992 Nobel Lecture,&rdquo; World Literature Today. 1993, 264.</p>

        <p>Learn more about <a class="link" href="{{ '/people/#dberg' | url }}">David Berg</a> and Blackwood Imaging.</p>
      </div>

